    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">How much do insurance adjusters make? The honest answer is "it depends on which kind of adjuster you mean." A salaried auto adjuster at a carrier, a 1099 storm adjuster working hurricane claims and a public adjuster earning a percentage of settlements all hold the same basic job title, yet their pay looks completely different.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> The BLS reports a median salary of $76,790 per year ($36.92/hour) for claims adjusters, appraisers, examiners and investigators. Staff adjusters typically earn $50,000 to $90,000. Independent and CAT adjusters swing from $30,000 in slow years to $150,000+ in big storm years, and public adjusters commonly earn $50,000 to $100,000.</div>

<p>This guide breaks pay down by adjuster type, experience level and line of business, then explains the levers that move your income up or down. For a broader overview, start with our <a href="/">insurance adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="national-baseline">The national baseline: what the BLS says</h2>
<p>The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics groups adjusters with appraisers, examiners and investigators. As of May 2024, that group had:</p>
<ul>
<li>A median annual wage of <strong>$76,790</strong>, or <strong>$36.92 per hour</strong></li>
<li>About <strong>365,300 jobs</strong> nationwide in 2024</li>
<li>A projected employment decline of 5% from 2024 to 2034, mostly because simple claims are being automated</li>
<li>Thousands of openings each year anyway, driven by retirements and turnover</li>
</ul>
<p>The median is a useful anchor, but it mostly reflects salaried carrier employees. It does not capture the high and low ends of independent and public adjusting very well. You can read the full methodology in the <a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/claims-adjusters-appraisers-examiners-and-investigators.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook</a>.</p>

<h2 id="pay-by-adjuster-type">How much do insurance adjusters make by type?</h2>
<p>There are four main ways to work as an adjuster, and each has its own pay model.</p>

<h3>Staff (company) adjusters</h3>
<p>Staff adjusters are W-2 employees of an insurance carrier or third-party administrator. They earn a steady salary, usually with health insurance, a 401(k) match and paid time off. Field staff often get a company car, gas card, laptop and phone on top of that, which can add $8,000 to $15,000 of value a year. Typical salaries run from about $50,000 to $90,000, with senior and large-loss roles above $100,000.</p>

<h3>Independent adjusters (IAs)</h3>
<p>Independent adjusters are 1099 contractors who work claims for IA firms that the carriers hire. They are paid per claim on a fee schedule tied to the size of the loss, or sometimes a daily rate. There are no benefits, and you pay your own travel, tools and taxes. Income is lumpy: slow years can land at $30,000 to $60,000, while average active years commonly fall between $70,000 and $120,000.</p>

<h3>Catastrophe (CAT) adjusters</h3>
<p>CAT adjusters are usually independents who deploy after hurricanes, hail, tornadoes and wildfires. A deployment can last two weeks to several months, with 12-hour days and seven-day weeks. Earnings per deployment commonly run $15,000 to $60,000 or more. Experienced CAT adjusters in a heavy storm year can reach $150,000 to $250,000, but that is a high-end outcome, not a typical year. Try our <a href="/cat-deployment-calculator/">CAT deployment earnings calculator</a> to model a storm season.</p>

<h3>Public adjusters</h3>
<p>Public adjusters work for policyholders, not insurers. They earn a contingency fee, typically 10% to 20% of the settlement, and many states cap that percentage. Annual income commonly runs $50,000 to $100,000, and established PAs with steady commercial or large-loss work can pass $150,000. See our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/public-adjuster-salary/">public adjuster salary</a> breakdown for the fee math.</p>

