<tr><td>Public adjuster</td><td>Contingency fee</td><td>$50,000 - $150,000+</td><td>None (unless salaried at a firm)</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<h2 id="pay-by-experience">Pay by experience level</h2>
<p>For staff adjusters, experience is the most predictable driver of pay. A common progression looks like this:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Trainee / entry level (0-2 years):</strong> $45,000 to $60,000. You handle simple, low-severity claims under close supervision.</li>
<li><strong>Adjuster (2-5 years):</strong> $58,000 to $80,000. You carry a full caseload and higher authority limits.</li>
<li><strong>Senior adjuster (5+ years):</strong> $75,000 to $100,000. You take complex coverage questions, litigated files or larger losses.</li>
<li><strong>Large loss / complex claims specialist:</strong> $85,000 to $120,000+. Commercial property, major injury and high-dollar files.</li>
</ol>
<p>For independents, experience shows up differently. Seasoned IAs get deployed first, receive the bigger and more complex files, and close claims faster, all of which raises what each day is worth. New IAs often spend their first season or two on small claims while they build a reputation with firms. Our guide to <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/entry-level-claims-adjuster-salary/">entry-level claims adjuster salary</a> covers the first two years in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="pay-by-line">Pay by line of business</h2>
<p>What you adjust matters almost as much as who you work for. These are typical staff ranges based on industry estimates:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Auto / material damage:</strong> $50,000 to $72,000</li>
<li><strong>Desk property:</strong> $55,000 to $75,000</li>
<li><strong>Workers compensation:</strong> $55,000 to $82,000</li>
<li><strong>Bodily injury / casualty:</strong> $60,000 to $88,000</li>
<li><strong>Field property:</strong> $62,000 to $90,000</li>
<li><strong>Senior, complex or large loss:</strong> $85,000 to $120,000+</li>
</ul>
<p>Casualty and large-loss property pay more because the files involve bigger dollars, legal exposure and judgment calls that carriers cannot automate. Simple auto and small property claims are the work most exposed to photo-estimating apps and AI triage, which is part of why the BLS expects slow overall job growth.</p>

<h2 id="what-drives-pay">What drives adjuster pay up or down</h2>
<p>Beyond type and line, a handful of factors explain most of the gap between a $55,000 adjuster and a $110,000 one:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Licenses held.</strong> Adjusters licensed in several states, often starting with a Texas or Florida all-lines license, are eligible for more deployments and more job postings.</li>
<li><strong>Software skills.</strong> Fluency in Xactimate for property estimating is close to mandatory for field and CAT work.</li>
<li><strong>Location.</strong> Large metros and storm-prone states have more volume. Compare markets on our <a href="/salary-by-state/">insurance adjuster salary by state</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Willingness to travel.</strong> The biggest paydays go to people who can leave home for weeks on short notice.</li>
<li><strong>Claim complexity.</strong> Commercial, large-loss and litigated claims pay more per file and per hour.</li>
<li><strong>Weather.</strong> For IAs and CAT adjusters, a quiet hurricane season can cut income in half.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Many adjusters split the difference: they take a staff role for stable pay and training, earn Xactimate skills and a multi-state license, then move into independent or CAT work once they can handle a full desk without supervision.</div>

<h2 id="raising-your-income">How to raise what you make as an adjuster</h2>
<p>If you want to move toward the top of these ranges, focus on the things that firms and carriers actually pay a premium for:</p>
<ol>
<li>Get licensed in a home state plus reciprocal states so you can take work wherever it is.</li>
<li>Specialize in a line that resists automation, such as commercial property, large loss or bodily injury.</li>
<li>Build a reputation for clean, fast files. Cycle time and file quality are what IA firms and carriers measure.</li>
<li>Earn designations such as AIC or CPCU, which can support raises and promotions on the staff side.</li>
<li>Track your real take-home after expenses, not just gross fees, if you work as a 1099 contractor.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you do not have a license yet, an online pre-licensing course is the fastest first step. AdjustingOnline offers state courses, and the code "joe20" takes 20% off at <a href="https://www.adjustingonline.com/courses/" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">AdjustingOnline</a>.</p>

<h2 id="estimate-your-pay">How much do insurance adjusters make in your situation?</h2>
<p>Ranges only go so far. Your number depends on your state, line, experience and how much you are willing to travel. Plug your details into the <a href="/salary-calculator/">adjuster salary calculator</a> to get a personalized estimate, then compare it against staff, independent and CAT scenarios before you choose a path.</p>
